%I A007792 #33 Sep 23 2026 17:49:02
%S A007792 1,8,81,640,821,648,9031,14336,32805,6568,17051,51840,122461,72248,
%T A007792 66501,360448,92753,262440,586531,525440,731511,136408,303095,1161216,
%U A007792 1125625,979688,3720087,5779840,754349,532008,4329151,5242880,1381131,742024,7414451,20995200
%N A007792 a(n) = (1/n)*Sum_{x in (Z/nZ)^6} e^(2*Pi*i*det(circ(x))/n), the normalized Gauss sum of the 6 X 6 circulant determinant.
%C A007792 From _Zoltan Reti_, Sep 06 2026: (Start)
%C A007792 For a vector x = (x_0, ..., x_5) let circ(x) be the 6 X 6 circulant matrix whose (i,j) entry is x_{(j-i) mod 6}, so that each row is the previous row shifted one place to the right. Its determinant is a form of degree 6 in 6 variables, and it is the product of two 3 X 3 circulant determinants: with A = x_0+x_3, B = x_1+x_4, C = x_2+x_5 and A' = x_0-x_3, B' = x_4-x_1, C' = x_2-x_5, det(circ(x)) = (A^3+B^3+C^3-3*A*B*C) * (A'^3+B'^3+C'^3-3*A'*B'*C'), each factor splitting again as (A+B+C)*(A^2+B^2+C^2-A*B-B*C-C*A). Put that determinant in the exponent of an additive character and average over all residue vectors modulo n. This is a multiple Gauss sum: its exponent is a form in six variables, in place of the single square of the classical one-variable Gauss sum.
%C A007792 a(n) values are positive integers. The sequence has no zeros, and the reason is that 6 is not a prime power: for d = 3, 4, 5 the single ramified prime p has a(p) = 0, whereas here both ramified cells a(2) = 8 and a(3) = 81 are nonzero and every other prime-power value is positive. The a(n) function is multiplicative, but not strictly multiplicative.
%C A007792 At a prime p > 3 the value depends only on p mod 6: a(p) = p^5-(p-1)^5 if p == 1 (mod 6), when u^6-1 splits completely over GF(p); a(p) = p^5-(p-1)^3*(p+1)^2 if p == 5 (mod 6), when the factor degrees are 1, 1, 2, 2.
%C A007792 There are two ramified primes, p = 2 and p = 3. Above the first cell each wild column is a fixed power of p times a quadratic in the exponent, and at p = 2 the quadratic depends on the parity of k: a(2) = 8, a(2^k) = 2^(4*k-4)*(9*k^2-30*k+64) for even k >= 2 and a(2^k) = 2^(4*k-4)*(9*k^2-60*k+155) for odd k >= 3; a(3) = 81, a(3^k) = (2*k^2-8*k+13)*3^(4*k) for k >= 2. The cofactors are a(2^k)/2^(4*k-4) = 40, 56, 88, 80, 208, 176, 400, 344, ... and a(3^k)/3^(4*k) = 5, 7, 13, 23, 37, 55, 77, 103, ... for k = 2, 3, 4, ... The parity split at p = 2 is real: no single quadratic fits both parities. (End)
%D A007792 B. C. Berndt, R. J. Evans and K. S. Williams, Gauss and Jacobi Sums, Wiley, 1998, p. 549.
%H A007792 Zoltan Reti, <a href="/A007792/b007792.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A007792 Zoltan Reti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21969894">The Multiple Gauss Sums Behind A007789-A007792</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A007792 Zoltan Reti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21970261">Multiple Gauss Sums Generated by Circulant Matrices</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A007792 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circulant_matrix">Circulant matrix</a>.
%H A007792 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauss_sum">Gauss sum</a>.
%F A007792 From _Zoltan Reti_, Sep 06 2026: (Start)
%F A007792 Multiplicative: a(n1*n2) = a(n1)*a(n2) for gcd(n1,n2) = 1.
%F A007792 If p == 1 (mod 6):
%F A007792   a(p^k) = p^(5*k) - (p-1)^5 * p^(4*(k-1)) * Sum_{j=0..k-1} binomial(j+4,4)*p^(k-1-j).
%F A007792 If p == 5 (mod 6):
%F A007792   a(p^k) = p^(5*k) - (p-1)*(p^2-1)^2 * p^(4*(k-1)) * Sum_{j=0..k-1} binomial(floor(j/2)+2,2)*p^(k-1-j).
%F A007792 If p = 2:
%F A007792   a(2) = 2^3;
%F A007792   a(2^k) = 2^(4*k-4)*(9*k^2-30*k+64) for even k >= 2;
%F A007792   a(2^k) = 2^(4*k-4)*(9*k^2-60*k+155) for odd k >= 3.
%F A007792 If p = 3:
%F A007792   a(3) = 3^4; a(3^k) = (2*k^2-8*k+13)*3^(4*k) for k >= 2.
%F A007792 (End)
%e A007792 a(7) = 9031: here 7 == 1 (mod 6), so u^6-1 splits completely over GF(7) and a(7) = 7^5 - 6^6/6 = 16807 - 7776 = 9031.
%e A007792 a(5) = 821: here 5 == 5 (mod 6), the factor degrees are 1, 1, 2, 2, and a(5) = 5^5 - 4*4*24*24/4 = 3125 - 2304 = 821.
%e A007792 a(10) = 6568: 10 = 2*5, so a(10) = a(2)*a(5) = 8*821 = 6568 by multiplicativity.
%e A007792 a(4) = 640 = 2^7*5 and a(9) = 32805 = 3^8*5: at a ramified prime the second cell is p^(2*d-m-2)*(p^m - U), with m = d/p and U the number of units of GF(p)[Z/m], so 2^7*(2^3-3) and 3^8*(3^2-4).
%t A007792 dv[dd_, p_] := Sort[Flatten[Table[With[{f = MultiplicativeOrder[p, e]}, Table[f, {EulerPhi[e]/f}]], {e, Divisors[dd]}]]];
%t A007792 gt[dd_, p_, k_] := Module[{fs = dv[dd, p], pr, T, z}, pr = PadRight[CoefficientList[Normal[Series[Product[(1 - p^-f)/(1 - (z/p)^f), {f, fs}], {z, 0, k}]], z], k + 1]; T[a_] := 1 - Sum[pr[[m + 1]], {m, 0, a - 1}]; p^((dd - 1)*k)*(p*T[k] - T[k - 1])/(p - 1)];
%t A007792 g[p_, k_] := Which[p == 2, If[k == 1, 8, 2^(4 k - 4)*If[EvenQ[k], 9 k^2 - 30 k + 64, 9 k^2 - 60 k + 155]], p == 3, If[k == 1, 81, (2 k^2 - 8 k + 13) 3^(4 k)], True, gt[6, p, k]];
%t A007792 a[n_] := If[n == 1, 1, Times @@ (g @@@ FactorInteger[n])];
%t A007792 Table[a[n], {n, 1, 36}]
